====================================================================================================
PART 1 -- where the 1/6 comes from (pinned from the LaTeX source of arXiv:1611.02269v2)
====================================================================================================
  a_M = (d-3)/((d-2)(d-1)) a0   [eq (1.7)];  d=4 => 1/6.  The 6 factorizes as 3 x 2:
    * (d-1) = 3 : the volume-law normalization V0 = 4G.hbar.L/(d-1) [eq (2.14)] -- ball volume
                  V = A.r/(d-1) [eq (2.13)] + boundary condition S_DE(L) = A(L)/4G.hbar [eq (2.12)].
                  Pure 3-spatial-dimension geometry once the volume-law POSTULATE is granted.
    * (d-2)/(d-3) = 2 : the ADM/Gauss conversion Sigma = (d-2)/(d-3) g/8piG [eq (1.6)] -- an
                  uncontested Newtonian/GR factor.
  Chain: S_M = 2 pi M r/hbar [eq (4.28), Bekenstein/Wald -- derived] -> V_M = (8piG/a0) M r/(d-1)
  [eq (4.33)] -> Eshelby inclusion condition INT eps^2 dV <= V_M [Sec 7.1; EQUALITY assumed] ->
  Sigma_D = (a0/8piG) eps [shear modulus a0^2/16piG, fixed by the self-energy match, Sec 6] ->
  INT (8piG Sigma_D/a0)^2 dV = ((d-2)/(d-1)) OINT (Phi_B/a0) n.dA [eq (7.36)] ->
  INT G M_D^2/r'^2 dr' = M_B a0 r/6 [eq (7.40), THE main result] -> g_D = sqrt(a0 g_B/6) [eq (7.43)].
  => GIVEN the postulates, 1/6 is FORCED (zero tunable freedom, zero data input). The freedom lives
     in the postulates themselves: (P1) volume-law dS entropy; (P2) linear-in-r S_M interpolation;
     (P3) the elastic dictionary; (P4) equality saturation of the strain inequality; (P5) spherical/
     static/isolated. P1+P3+P4 are the contested part (Dai-Stojkovic 1710.00946 vs Yoon 2003.03198).
====================================================================================================
PART 2 -- the four cross-products (both footings x both coefficients), arXiv-pinned
====================================================================================================
  cH0      = 6.5484e-10 m/s^2   (measured-H0 / rho_total footing; Verlinde eq (1.2) face value)
  cH_L     = 5.4198e-10 m/s^2   (pure-Lambda / rho_DE footing; Verlinde's OWN Sec-8 caveat:
             'a0 should actually be defined in terms of the dark energy density, or the value
             of the cosmological constant... it takes a slightly different value')
                             /6 (Verlinde)  /Z=5.789 (framework)   ratio 6/Z
  cH0 footing                   1.0914e-10            1.1312e-10      0.9648
  cH_Lambda footing             9.0330e-11            9.3625e-11      0.9648

  coefficient gap: 6 vs Z = sqrt(32pi/3) = 5.7888  ->  3.65% (a_M is 3.65% LOW of a0_fw
  on the same footing). Footing gap (cH0 vs cH_L) = 1/sqrt(Omega_L) - 1 = 20.8% -- the
  footing fork is 6x LARGER than the 6-vs-Z fork. The footing dominates the comparison.
  External anchor: Yoon+22 (2206.11685) / Diez-Tejedor+18 (1612.06282) 'quasi-de-Sitter' EG uses
  a0 = 5.41e-10 = cH_Lambda (the repo's own footing) and finds it fits SPARC BETTER than cH0
  (offset -0.027 vs -0.060 dex) -- an independent, non-framework group landing on the rho_DE branch.

====================================================================================================
PART 3 -- is 3.65% distinguishable? (against every banked error scale)
====================================================================================================
  error scale                                                                   size gap/scale
  RAR stat error (McGaugh+16 1609.05917: 1.20+/-0.02e-10)                       1.7%      2.19
  RAR M/L systematic (same paper: +/-0.24e-10)                                 20.0%      0.18
  fit-METRIC swing, fixed Upsilon=0.70 (banked: 8.5e-11..1.3e-10)              41.9%      0.09
  SHAPE dependence (EG-shape fit prefers a_M ~30% below cH0/6, 1909.01734/2206.11685)   30.0%      0.12
  footing fork cH0 vs cH_L (the rho_total/rho_DE branch)                       20.8%      0.18
  The 3.65% gap is 0.18x the M/L systematic, 0.09x the metric swing, 0.12x the shape systematic.
  Only the formal 1.7% stat error could nominally split it (2.2 sigma) -- but that error bar is itself
  convention-fiction: the central value moves ~40% with the weighting and ~30% with the assumed shape.
  To split 6 from Z at 3 sigma needs sigma(a0) < 1.2% TOTAL -- below even the stat-only error,
  and ~20x below the honest systematic floor. Per the footing audit's discrimination table (framework vs
  thermal 8.2% needs <2.7%), this pair is the LEAST distinguishable coefficient pair in the corpus.
  Deep-MOND amplitude check: v_f = (a_M G M_B)^(1/4) shifts by only (Z/6)^(1/4)-1 = -0.89% -- BTFR
  zero-point scatter/normalization uncertainty (~10%+) is ~10x larger. NOT DISTINGUISHABLE, any convention.
